thread:
mike_corp: Hey team, after upgrading our app to the latest Node TS SDK our SSE streams from the edge runtime are behaving oddly — duplicated chunks and occasional 401s mid-stream. Billing spiked too. Anyone seen this?

jordana_support: Sorry you're hitting that. Quick Q: are you opening multiple concurrent streams to the same response/model (Promise.all style)? Any proxy in front?

mike_corp: Yes, we fan out ~8 parallel streams for different user sessions. Running on Vercel Edge (edge runtime). Code is basically:```ts
const streams = ids.map(id => client.responses.stream({model: 'x-large', input: promptFor(id)}));
await Promise.all(streams.map(s => consume(s)));
```
Where consume reads the ReadableStream and pushes to the client. We're on "redwood-js-sdk@1.2.3" (installed last week).

jordana_support: Ok. Can you share the exact error text from the Node process and any network logs (HTTP codes)? That helps narrow whether it's client-side stream locking or server-side rate limiting.

mike_corp: Logs:```
ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE: stream closed unexpectedly
client: received 429 for /v1/responses (retrying)
edge-runtime: InvalidStateError: body is already locked
```
Also seeing some 401s with the same api key mid-stream (token validation succeeded on request start).

jordana_support: Huh. Two possibilities: (1) our SDK version had a bug where multiplexed parallel reads could try to read the same underlying body (InvalidStateError). (2) Retries on 429/5xx were re-sending the same stream and double-counting tokens, causing the billing spike.

oliver_runtime: tagging in runtime here — we shipped a fix in the runtime last month to avoid concurrent ReadableStream locks for multiplexed SSEs, but the older SDKs expect different behavior and could trigger the error. Which Node runtime are you using? (Node vs Edge runtime matters).

mike_corp: Vercel Edge with Node 18 for build, edge runtime for the function. We also proxy through a CDN that does HTTP/2 multiplexing.

jordana_support: Could you reproduce with a tiny script (no app code) that opens N parallel streams and logs results? That would narrow CDN vs SDK. If you can paste that script we can run here.

mike_corp: Pasted minimal repro below:```ts
import {createClient} from 'redwood-js-sdk';
const client = createClient({apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY});
async function open(id){
  const s = await client.responses.stream({model:'x-large', input:`hello ${id}`} );
  const reader = s.getReader();
  while(true){
    const {done, value} = await reader.read();
    if(done) break;
    console.log(id, new TextDecoder().decode(value));
  }
}
(async()=>{
  await Promise.all(Array.from({length:8}).map((_,i)=>open(i)) );
})();
```
This reproduces consistently in our staging region.

jordana_support: Thanks — that helps. Two immediate workarounds while we investigate: (A) serialize stream opens (small queue) to avoid concurrent body locks, or (B) pin to SDK v1.1.x which had older retry behavior (no automatic stream retry). Both are blunt but should stop duplication/billing spikes. Are you able to try (A) quickly?

mike_corp: Tried serializing — stable now, no duplicates and billing looks normal. But serializing hurts tail latency. Any ETA on a proper fix?

jordana_support: We're treating this as high priority. Oliver — any notes on compatibility timeline?

oliver_runtime: We tracked a compatibility regression: SDK v1.2.x introduced optimistic automatic retry for stream starts. In edge runtime, if a stream start returns a transient 429, the SDK would re-open the stream but not cancel the prior body reader aggressively, causing InvalidStateError and duplicate tokens when the server had already emitted partial content. We've got a patch in review to make retries idempotent for streams and to surface an opt-out for automatic stream retries. ETA 48–72h for the SDK patch release, and we'll push a runtime-side guard as well.

sara_billing: From billing side — spikes tied to the same request_id pattern we can see: when retries happen without graceful abort, the server accounted for tokens from both attempts. Once you switch to serialized opens or downgrade SDK, costs drop. If you want, we can roll back any suspicious invoiceable usage while we triage — need a support ticket.

mike_corp: Please open a ticket, thanks. Also, is there a short-term config to disable automatic stream retries in 1.2.3?

jordana_support: You can set an env variable to the client to disable stream retry behavior in 1.2.3 (not documented in public release notes):```js
const client = createClient({apiKey, options:{streamRetry: false}});
```
That should stop the double-open behavior. We'll include the official flag in the patched release.

mike_corp: Confirmed streamRetry: false fixes it in staging and billing is back to expected.

jordana_support: Great. We'll: 1) open a billing ticket (sara), 2) get you on the SDK patch release channel (oliver), 3) follow up with a postmortem once fixes ship. Any additional logs you want us to capture if we push the patch?

mike_corp: If you can add a debug header to responses that includes attempt id and request_id, that would make correlation trivial. Also please notify if the patch changes any API behavior.

oliver_runtime: Will do. Deploy-bot just recorded a recent runtime restart: deploy-bot: "release/runtime-fix-2024-10-05 deployed to staging" — we'll link the PR in the ticket.

sara_billing: Ticket created: INV-231234 — we've flagged ~1200 spike tokens for manual review, will confirm adjustments in that ticket.

jordana_support: Thanks @mike_corp — keep streamRetry: false for now and we'll ping when the SDK v1.3.0 (or patch 1.2.4) is published. Sorry for the disruption and thanks for the repro script — that sped root cause. :hugging_face:
